Node.js provides the built-in 'http' module to create a web server. You can define routes, handle requests, and send responses. The server listens on a specified port for incoming HTTP requests.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const http = require('http');
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.statusCode = 200;
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/plain');
  res.end('Hello, World!\n');
});
server.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));
    </pre>
    Run this and visit 'http://localhost:3000' to see the response.

Express.js is a minimal and flexible web framework for Node.js, simplifying the creation of web servers and APIs. It provides middleware, routing, and utilities for handling HTTP requests and responses.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.get('/', (req, res) => res.send('Hello, Express!'));
app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));
    </pre>
    Install Express with 'npm install express' and run this code.

Middleware functions in Express are functions that execute during the request-response cycle, with access to the request (req), response (res), and next middleware (next). They can modify requests, validate data, or handle errors.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log('Request received at:', new Date());
  next();
});
app.get('/', (req, res) => res.send('Hello!'));
app.listen(3000);
    </pre>
    This logs the timestamp of each request.

Express provides 'express.json()' middleware to parse incoming JSON requests. You can access the parsed data via 'req.body' in your routes.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.post('/data', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ received: req.body });
});
app.listen(3000);
    </pre>
    Send a POST request with JSON to '/data' to see the response.

Mongoose is an ODM (Object Data Modeling) library for MongoDB and Node.js, simplifying database interactions. You connect to MongoDB using a connection string and define schemas for data.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
mongoose.connect('mongodb://localhost/mydb', { useNewUrlParser: true });
const User = mongoose.model('User', new mongoose.Schema({ name: String }));
const user = new User({ name: 'Alice' });
user.save().then(() => console.log('User saved'));
    </pre>
    Install Mongoose with 'npm install mongoose'.

A REST API in Node.js is typically built with Express, defining endpoints for CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete). Routes handle HTTP methods like GET, POST, PUT, DELETE.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
let users = [];
app.get('/users', (req, res) => res.json(users));
app.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  users.push(req.body);
  res.status(201).json(req.body);
});
app.listen(3000);
    </pre>
    This creates a simple REST API for users.

Environment variables store configuration settings (e.g., API keys) securely. The 'dotenv' package loads variables from a '.env' file into 'process.env'.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// .env file
PORT=3000
// index.js
require('dotenv').config();
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.listen(process.env.PORT, () => console.log('Server on port ' + process.env.PORT));
    </pre>
    Install dotenv with 'npm install dotenv'.

Node.js uses an event-driven, non-blocking I/O model, allowing asynchronous operations (e.g., file reading, HTTP requests) to run without blocking the main thread. Callbacks, Promises, and Async/Await handle async code.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const fs = require('fs');
fs.readFile('file.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  console.log(data);
});
    </pre>
    This reads a file asynchronously.

Async/Await simplifies asynchronous code by allowing you to write it like synchronous code. Functions marked 'async' return Promises, and 'await' pauses execution until the Promise resolves.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const fs = require('fs').promises;
async function readFile() {
  try {
    const data = await fs.readFile('file.txt', 'utf8');
    console.log(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
  }
}
readFile();
    </pre>
    This reads a file using Async/Await.

WebSocket starts with an HTTP handshake (using the 'Upgrade' header) to establish a persistent connection. Once established, both client and server can send messages anytime, ideal for real-time apps like chat or live notifications.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });
wss.on('connection', ws => {
  ws.on('message', message => console.log('Received:', message));
  ws.send('Welcome to WebSocket!');
});
    </pre>
    Install 'ws' with 'npm install ws' and run this server.

The 'ws' library is commonly used to create a WebSocket server in Node.js. It handles connections, messages, and events like 'open' and 'close'.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });
wss.on('connection', ws => {
  ws.on('message', message => {
    ws.send('Echo: ' + message);
  });
});
    </pre>
    This echoes messages back to the client.

To set up a WebSocket server, install the 'ws' package and create a server that listens for connections. Clients connect using the WebSocket API in the browser.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Server (server.js)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });
wss.on('connection', ws => {
  ws.send('Connected!');
});
// Client (HTML)
const ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:8080');
ws.onmessage = event => console.log(event.data);
    </pre>
    Run 'npm install ws' and start the server.

A WebSocket client (e.g., in a browser) connects to a server using 'new WebSocket(url)'. It listens for events like 'open', 'message', 'close', and sends data with 'socket.send()'.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:8080');
ws.onopen = () => ws.send('Hello, Server!');
ws.onmessage = event => console.log('Received:', event.data);
ws.onclose = () => console.log('Disconnected');
    </pre>
    This client connects and communicates with a WebSocket server.

JSON Web Tokens (JWT) are used for secure authentication. A token is generated on the server, signed with a secret, and sent to the client. The client includes it in requests to verify identity.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const token = jwt.sign({ userId: 1 }, 'secret');
  res.json({ token });
});
app.listen(3000);
    </pre>
    Install 'jsonwebtoken' with 'npm install jsonwebtoken'.

Redis is an in-memory data store used for caching, session management, or real-time analytics. In Node.js, the 'redis' package connects to a Redis server.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const redis = require('redis');
const client = redis.createClient();
client.set('key', 'value', redis.print);
client.get('key', (err, value) => console.log(value));
    </pre>
    Install 'redis' with 'npm install redis' and run a Redis server.

The EventEmitter class enables event-driven programming in Node.js. Objects can emit named events, and listeners can handle them, useful for asynchronous operations.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const EventEmitter = require('events');
const emitter = new EventEmitter();
emitter.on('greet', name => console.log('Hello, ' + name));
emitter.emit('greet', 'Alice');
    </pre>
    This emits and handles a custom 'greet' event.

Errors in Node.js can be handled using try-catch for synchronous code, error-first callbacks, or Promise error handling. Express provides middleware for centralized error handling.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.get('/error', (req, res, next) => {
  next(new Error('Something went wrong'));
});
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  res.status(500).send(err.message);
});
app.listen(3000);
    </pre>
    This handles errors in an Express app.

Both schedule callbacks, but 'process.nextTick()' runs before the next event loop phase, potentially delaying I/O, while 'setImmediate()' runs in the next event loop cycle, after I/O.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
process.nextTick(() => console.log('Next tick'));
setImmediate(() => console.log('Immediate'));
console.log('Main');
    </pre>
    Output: Main, Next tick, Immediate.

The Node.js event loop, implemented via libuv, runs through a fixed set of phases on every iteration, each with its own FIFO queue of callbacks: <strong>timers</strong> (executes setTimeout/setInterval callbacks whose threshold has elapsed), <strong>pending callbacks</strong> (executes I/O callbacks deferred from the previous loop iteration, e.g. certain TCP errors), <strong>poll</strong> (retrieves new I/O events and executes their callbacks — this is where most work happens, and the loop can block here waiting for I/O if nothing else is scheduled), <strong>check</strong> (executes setImmediate callbacks, which always run right after poll), and <strong>close callbacks</strong> (e.g. socket.on('close', ...)). Separate from these phases, Node maintains two microtask-like queues that drain completely between every phase transition and between every callback within a phase: <strong>process.nextTick()</strong> callbacks run first, then <strong>Promise</strong> microtasks (.then/.catch/.finally and await continuations). Because the nextTick queue is checked after literally every callback, a recursive process.nextTick() call can starve the event loop and block I/O indefinitely — this is why process.nextTick is reserved for very small, deliberate uses rather than general async scheduling.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
console.log('start');
setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout (timers phase)'), 0);
setImmediate(() => console.log('immediate (check phase)'));
process.nextTick(() => console.log('nextTick (microtask, drains first)'));
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('promise (microtask, drains second)'));
console.log('end');
// Output:
// start
// end
// nextTick (microtask, drains first)
// promise (microtask, drains second)
// timeout (timers phase)      <- order vs immediate depends on context
// immediate (check phase)
    </pre>
    Inside an I/O callback specifically, setImmediate is guaranteed to run before any setTimeout(fn, 0), because the poll phase always transitions to check before it wraps back around to timers.

Streams are Node's abstraction for handling data that arrives incrementally rather than all at once, letting you process gigabyte-sized files or long-lived socket connections using constant memory instead of buffering everything. There are four core types: <strong>Readable</strong> (a source you read from, e.g. fs.createReadStream), <strong>Writable</strong> (a destination you write to, e.g. fs.createWriteStream or an HTTP response), <strong>Duplex</strong> (both readable and writable, independently, e.g. a TCP socket), and <strong>Transform</strong> (a duplex stream that transforms data as it passes through, e.g. zlib.createGzip). <strong>Backpressure</strong> is what happens when a writable destination can't keep up with an upstream readable source — if you ignore it and just call write() in a loop, the writable's internal buffer grows unbounded and can crash the process with an out-of-memory error. write() returns false when its internal buffer is over its highWaterMark, signaling that the source should pause until a drain event fires. The pipe() method, and pipeline() for proper error handling, automatically pause and resume the source stream for you, which is why piping is the idiomatic way to move data between streams instead of manually forwarding data events.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const { pipeline } = require('stream');
const fs = require('fs');
const zlib = require('zlib');

// pipeline() wires backpressure between all three streams AND
// forwards/cleans up on error -- unlike chained .pipe(), a single
// failure anywhere doesn't leave dangling file descriptors.
pipeline(
  fs.createReadStream('big-input.log'),
  zlib.createGzip(),
  fs.createWriteStream('big-input.log.gz'),
  (err) => {
    if (err) console.error('Pipeline failed:', err);
    else console.log('Pipeline succeeded');
  }
);
    </pre>
    Never forward chunks by hand with readable.on('data', chunk => writable.write(chunk)) in production code — it ignores the false return value from write() and defeats backpressure, which is exactly the memory-growth bug streams exist to prevent.

Node.js runs your JavaScript on a single thread, so a CPU-bound task, like heavy computation, image processing, or synchronous parsing of huge payloads, blocks the event loop and stalls every other request until it finishes. Both cluster and worker_threads exist to work around this, but for different goals. The <strong>cluster</strong> module forks multiple full copies of your Node process, each with separate V8 instances, memory, and event loops, that share the same server port via the OS; the primary process load-balances incoming connections across the worker processes. It's the classic way to use all CPU cores for an HTTP server, and a crash in one worker doesn't take down the others, but workers can't share memory directly (only IPC message passing), so it doesn't help a single CPU-heavy computation finish faster. <strong>worker_threads</strong> instead runs JavaScript on separate threads within the same process, each with its own V8 instance and event loop but with the option to share memory directly via SharedArrayBuffer — this is the right tool for offloading one expensive synchronous computation off the main thread so it doesn't block incoming requests, without paying the overhead of a whole new OS process.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// worker_threads: offload a CPU-bound task without blocking the
// main thread's event loop (and therefore other requests).
const { Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData } = require('worker_threads');

if (isMainThread) {
  function runHeavyTask(n) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const worker = new Worker(__filename, { workerData: n });
      worker.on('message', resolve);
      worker.on('error', reject);
    });
  }
  runHeavyTask(40).then(result => console.log('fib(40) =', result));
} else {
  function fib(n) { return n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2); }
  parentPort.postMessage(fib(workerData));
}
    </pre>
    Rule of thumb: use cluster, or a process manager like PM2 in cluster mode, to scale an I/O-bound HTTP server across CPU cores; use worker_threads to move one specific CPU-heavy function off the main thread.

A memory leak in Node.js is memory that's still reachable, so the garbage collector won't reclaim it, but is no longer actually needed by the application — over time the process's heap grows until it hits the V8 heap limit and crashes, or the OS kills it for exceeding a container memory limit. The standard workflow: run the process with <strong>--inspect</strong> (or --inspect-brk) and connect Chrome DevTools' Memory tab, take a heap snapshot, exercise the suspected leaking code path repeatedly, take a second snapshot, and use the Comparison view to see which object types grew and didn't shrink back — the retainer tree then shows exactly what's still holding a reference to those objects. In production, tools like clinic.js (clinic heapprofiler) or taking periodic heap snapshots under load can catch leaks that only show up over hours. The most common real-world leak sources are: an <strong>unbounded in-memory cache</strong> with no eviction policy or TTL; <strong>event listeners that are added but never removed</strong>, especially on long-lived EventEmitters (Node even warns 'MaxListenersExceededWarning' at 10 listeners by default); <strong>closures capturing large objects</strong> longer than intended; and <strong>global arrays or queues</strong> that are pushed to but never drained on an error path.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Leak: cache grows forever, nothing is ever evicted
const cache = {};
app.get('/user/:id', async (req, res) => {
  if (!cache[req.params.id]) {
    cache[req.params.id] = await db.findUser(req.params.id);
  }
  res.json(cache[req.params.id]);
});

// Fix: bounded cache with TTL/LRU eviction (e.g. via the 'lru-cache' package)
const LRU = require('lru-cache');
const cache2 = new LRU({ max: 500, ttl: 1000 * 60 * 5 });
app.get('/user/:id', async (req, res) => {
  let user = cache2.get(req.params.id);
  if (!user) {
    user = await db.findUser(req.params.id);
    cache2.set(req.params.id, user);
  }
  res.json(user);
});
    </pre>
    node --inspect server.js, then chrome://inspect in Chrome, is usually the fastest way to go from "memory keeps climbing" to the exact object graph holding it alive.

In a production Express app, middleware should be layered in a deliberate order: request-scoped concerns first (a request-id generator, structured request logging), then security (helmet, CORS, rate limiting), then body parsing, then authentication, then authorization, then your route handlers, and finally, registered last after all routes, a 404 handler and a centralized error-handling middleware. Express recognizes an error-handling middleware specifically because it declares four parameters (err, req, res, next); any error thrown in a synchronous handler, or passed to next(err) from an async one, skips every remaining normal middleware and jumps straight to the nearest error handler. Centralizing error handling in one place means every route can just throw or call next(err) with a typed error and trust that logging, status-code mapping, and the client-facing error shape are handled consistently in exactly one place, instead of every route reinventing its own try/catch and response formatting.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode = 500) {
    super(message);
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    this.isOperational = true; // vs. an unexpected bug
  }
}

// Wrap async route handlers so a rejected promise reaches next(err)
// automatically -- Express does NOT do this for you by default.
const asyncHandler = (fn) => (req, res, next) => Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);

app.get('/orders/:id', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const order = await db.findOrder(req.params.id);
  if (!order) throw new AppError('Order not found', 404);
  res.json(order);
}));

app.use((req, res) => res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' })); // 404s

// Centralized error handler -- MUST have 4 args and be registered last
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  const status = err.statusCode || 500;
  if (!err.isOperational) logger.error(err); // unexpected -> alert/log stack
  res.status(status).json({ error: err.isOperational ? err.message : 'Internal server error' });
});
    </pre>
    Distinguishing operational errors, like bad input or not-found, from programmer bugs matters: operational errors are safe to expose a clean message for, while an unexpected bug's raw message and stack should be logged internally but never leaked to the client.

A production API needs defense in depth across several layers. <strong>helmet</strong> sets a batch of security-related HTTP headers, like Content-Security-Policy, X-Content-Type-Options, and HSTS, in one call, closing off a class of header-based attacks with almost no effort. <strong>Rate limiting</strong> protects against brute-force login attempts and API abuse by capping requests per IP, user, or token in a time window. <strong>Input validation</strong> should reject malformed or unexpected input at the edge, before it reaches business logic — this also closes off NoSQL injection, since a raw, unvalidated request body passed straight into a Mongo query lets an attacker send an object as a password field to bypass a check; validating that fields are the expected primitive type neutralizes that class of attack, and sanitizer libraries strip dangerous keys defensively as a second layer. For <strong>JWT</strong>: always verify the signature, never trust an unverified decode; set a short expiry and use refresh tokens instead of long-lived access tokens; keep the signing secret outside source control; prefer an allowlist of algorithms and reject 'alg: none'; and put tokens in an httpOnly, secure, sameSite cookie rather than localStorage to reduce XSS exposure.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const helmet = require('helmet');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
const { z } = require('zod');
const mongoSanitize = require('express-mongo-sanitize');

app.use(helmet());
app.use(mongoSanitize()); // strips keys starting with '$' or containing '.'
app.use('/api/login', rateLimit({ windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, max: 10 }));

const loginSchema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  password: z.string().min(8),
});

app.post('/api/login', (req, res, next) => {
  const result = loginSchema.safeParse(req.body);
  if (!result.success) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid input' });
  // result.data.password is guaranteed to be a plain string here --
  // a NoSQL injection payload would already have failed validation.
  next();
}, loginHandler);
    </pre>
    None of these is a silver bullet on its own — the goal is layering them so a gap in one control doesn't turn into a full compromise by itself.

Redis is an in-memory key-value store commonly placed in front of a slower database to absorb read load and cut latency. The strategy you pick depends on your read/write patterns and tolerance for stale data. <strong>Cache-aside (lazy loading)</strong>: the app checks Redis first; on a miss it reads the database, writes the result into Redis with a TTL, and returns it — simple and only caches data that's actually requested, but the first request after an eviction always pays the full database latency. <strong>Write-through</strong>: every write goes to Redis and the database together, so the cache is never stale, at the cost of extra write latency. <strong>Write-behind (write-back)</strong>: writes go to Redis immediately and are flushed to the database asynchronously in batches — very fast writes, but risks data loss if Redis crashes before the flush. For invalidation, a short TTL is the simplest safety net, bounding how stale data can get even if you forget to invalidate, while explicit invalidation on writes keeps data fresher but requires finding every code path that mutates that data.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const redis = require('redis').createClient();

// Cache-aside with a TTL.
async function getProduct(id) {
  const key = 'product:' + id;
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  const product = await db.findProduct(id);
  await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(product), { EX: 300 }); // 5 min TTL
  return product;
}

// Explicit invalidation on write, so readers never see stale data
// for longer than it takes this update to complete.
async function updateProduct(id, changes) {
  const product = await db.updateProduct(id, changes);
  await redis.del('product:' + id);
  return product;
}
    </pre>
    In practice, most APIs default to cache-aside with a modest TTL for read-heavy, tolerant-of-a-few-seconds-staleness data, and reserve write-through for data where staleness is unacceptable, like account balances or inventory counts.

All three move data between services, but they fit different communication shapes. <strong>REST over HTTP/JSON</strong> is synchronous request/response, human-readable, cacheable by standard HTTP infrastructure, and universally supported — it's the default choice for public APIs and for internal calls where simplicity and debuggability matter more than raw throughput. <strong>gRPC</strong> is also synchronous request/response (plus streaming variants), but runs over HTTP/2 with Protocol Buffers instead of JSON: binary encoding is smaller and faster to serialize, the .proto contract generates strongly-typed client and server code in every language, and HTTP/2 multiplexing avoids head-of-line blocking — it's a common choice for internal, high-throughput microservice-to-microservice calls where both ends are services you control. <strong>Message queues</strong> like RabbitMQ, Kafka, or SQS flip the model to asynchronous and decoupled: a producer publishes an event and doesn't wait for a response, one or more consumers process it whenever they're ready — this is the right choice when the caller shouldn't block on the work finishing, when you need to buffer load spikes, or when multiple independent services need to react to the same event.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// REST: synchronous, simple, good for a public API
app.get('/api/orders/:id', async (req, res) => {
  res.json(await orderService.get(req.params.id));
});

// Message queue: async, decoupled -- the HTTP request returns
// immediately; downstream services react to the event independently.
app.post('/api/orders', async (req, res) => {
  const order = await orderService.create(req.body);
  await queue.publish('order.placed', { orderId: order.id });
  res.status(201).json(order); // doesn't wait for billing/inventory/email
});
    </pre>
    A common production architecture uses all three together: gRPC between internal services for speed, REST for the public-facing edge, and a queue for anything that's fire-and-forget or needs to fan out to multiple independent consumers.

When a container orchestrator or deployment tool stops an instance, during a rolling deploy, an autoscaling scale-down, or a crash restart, it sends SIGTERM and gives the process a grace period before force-killing it with SIGKILL. If the process exits immediately, any in-flight requests get dropped mid-response. A graceful shutdown handler catches SIGTERM, stops accepting new connections, lets in-flight requests finish, closes external resources like database pools and message-queue consumers, and only then exits, with a hard timeout fallback in case something hangs. Alongside that, orchestrators poll a <strong>health-check endpoint</strong> to decide whether to route traffic to an instance at all: a <strong>liveness</strong> check (is the process alive and not deadlocked) is usually a trivial 200 OK, while a <strong>readiness</strong> check (is the process ready to serve traffic) should actually verify its critical dependencies, since a Node process can be alive but unable to serve requests if its database connection dropped.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const server = app.listen(3000);

app.get('/healthz', (req, res) => res.status(200).send('ok')); // liveness
app.get('/readyz', async (req, res) => {                        // readiness
  try {
    await db.ping();
    res.status(200).send('ready');
  } catch {
    res.status(503).send('not ready');
  }
});

function shutdown(signal) {
  console.log(signal + ' received: closing server gracefully');
  server.close(async () => {
    await db.close();
    await redisClient.quit();
    console.log('Cleanup complete, exiting');
    process.exit(0);
  });
  // Force-exit if cleanup hangs longer than the orchestrator's grace period
  setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 10000).unref();
}

process.on('SIGTERM', () => shutdown('SIGTERM'));
process.on('SIGINT', () => shutdown('SIGINT'));
    </pre>
    Skipping graceful shutdown is a common cause of intermittent 502s during deploys — the load balancer keeps sending requests to an instance that's already been killed mid-response.

Structured logging means emitting logs as machine-parseable data, JSON objects with consistent field names, instead of free-text strings — a log aggregator can then filter, query, and alert on specific fields like level, userId, requestId, or durationMs instead of grepping text. Libraries like pino or winston make this the default output format and let you attach a consistent set of fields to every log line. <strong>Distributed tracing</strong> extends this idea across service boundaries: a single incoming request is assigned a <strong>trace id</strong>, and every service it touches creates a <strong>span</strong> tagged with that same trace id and a parent span id, recording its own start and end time and any errors. Propagating the trace id through HTTP headers, the W3C traceparent header is the standard now, and through message-queue metadata lets a tool like Jaeger, Zipkin, or an OpenTelemetry-compatible backend reassemble the whole request's path across services into one waterfall view — invaluable for finding which one of several downstream calls is actually responsible for a slow or failed request.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const pino = require('pino');
const logger = pino();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  req.id = req.headers['x-request-id'] || crypto.randomUUID();
  req.log = logger.child({ requestId: req.id, path: req.path });
  res.setHeader('x-request-id', req.id); // propagate to the client / next hop
  next();
});

app.get('/api/orders/:id', async (req, res) => {
  req.log.info({ orderId: req.params.id }, 'fetching order');
  const order = await orderService.get(req.params.id); // pass req.id downstream too
  res.json(order);
});
    </pre>
    Even without a full tracing backend, just propagating and logging a single correlation id through every service touched by a request makes it possible to pull every log line for one failed request out of a mountain of concurrent traffic.

Opening a new database connection, with its TCP handshake, TLS negotiation, and authentication, is relatively expensive, tens of milliseconds, so opening one per request would add real latency and could exhaust the database's max-connections limit under load. A <strong>connection pool</strong> opens a fixed number of connections up front, or on demand up to a max, and hands them out to queries as needed, returning each one to the pool when the query finishes instead of closing it. Sizing the pool matters in both directions: too small and requests queue up waiting for a free connection under load, visible as rising p99 latency even though the database itself isn't overloaded; too large and you can exceed the database's own connection limit, or, in a horizontally scaled deployment with many app instances each opening their own pool, accidentally hit far more total connections than the database allows. A common mistake in serverless deployments is creating a brand-new pool on every invocation instead of reusing one across warm invocations, which defeats pooling entirely.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const { Pool } = require('pg');

// Create ONE pool per process, at module load time -- reused across
// every request, not recreated per-request.
const pool = new Pool({
  host: process.env.DB_HOST,
  max: 10,                    // max simultaneous connections from this instance
  idleTimeoutMillis: 30000,   // release idle connections back after 30s
  connectionTimeoutMillis: 5000, // fail fast instead of queuing forever
});

app.get('/api/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const { rows } = await pool.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [req.params.id]);
  res.json(rows[0]);
});

process.on('SIGTERM', () => pool.end());
    </pre>
    A good starting point: the number of app instances times the pool's max size should stay comfortably under the database's max_connections, leaving headroom for admin and migration connections and other services sharing the same database.

All three, from the child_process module, launch a separate OS process, but they differ in how they handle output and what they're optimized for. <strong>exec</strong> runs a command through a shell, buffers all of its stdout/stderr in memory, and invokes a callback with the full output once the process exits — convenient for short-lived commands with small output, but risky for anything that can produce a lot of output, since it's held entirely in memory and exec enforces a maxBuffer limit that throws if exceeded. <strong>spawn</strong> also runs a command but streams stdout/stderr as Readable streams instead of buffering them, so it's the right choice for long-running processes or ones with large or unbounded output. <strong>fork</strong> is a specialized version of spawn specifically for launching another Node.js script as a child process, and it automatically sets up an IPC channel so the parent and child can exchange structured messages with process.send() and on('message') — it's commonly used to run a CPU-heavy task in a fully isolated process, similar in goal to worker_threads but with process-level isolation instead of thread-level.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const { spawn, fork } = require('child_process');

// spawn: stream output instead of buffering the whole thing in memory
const grep = spawn('grep', ['ERROR', 'app.log']);
grep.stdout.on('data', (chunk) => process.stdout.write(chunk));
grep.on('close', (code) => console.log('grep exited with ' + code));

// fork: run another Node script with a built-in IPC message channel
const child = fork('./worker-script.js');
child.send({ task: 'resize-image', path: '/tmp/photo.png' });
child.on('message', (result) => console.log('Worker finished:', result));
    </pre>
    Rule of thumb: exec for a quick shell command with small output, spawn for anything long-running or high-output, fork specifically when the child is another Node.js file you want to message back and forth with.

When one service calls another over the network, that call can fail transiently, like a blip or a timeout, or persistently, when the downstream service is down or overloaded, and naive handling of either case causes cascading failures. <strong>Retries</strong> handle transient failures: retry a failed call a bounded number of times with <strong>exponential backoff and jitter</strong>, waiting progressively longer between attempts with randomness added so many clients don't retry in lockstep and hammer the recovering service simultaneously. Retries should only be applied to idempotent operations — blindly retrying a non-idempotent write like charging a credit card can double-charge a user. The <strong>circuit breaker</strong> pattern protects against persistent failures: it tracks the failure rate of calls to a downstream service, and once failures cross a threshold it opens the circuit, failing fast locally without even attempting the network call for a cooldown period instead of piling up slow, doomed requests and their retries. After the cooldown, it moves to a half-open state, allows a trial request through, and closes the circuit again if that succeeds.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const CircuitBreaker = require('opossum');

async function callInventoryService(sku) {
  const res = await fetch('http://inventory-svc/stock/' + sku, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(2000) });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Inventory service error: ' + res.status);
  return res.json();
}

const breaker = new CircuitBreaker(callInventoryService, {
  timeout: 3000,            // treat as a failure if it takes longer than this
  errorThresholdPercentage: 50, // open the circuit at 50% failure rate
  resetTimeout: 10000,      // try a single request again after 10s
});

breaker.fallback(() => ({ inStock: null, source: 'fallback' })); // degrade gracefully

app.get('/api/products/:sku', async (req, res) => {
  res.json(await breaker.fire(req.params.sku));
});
    </pre>
    Together, retries handle the case where a call failed once, and circuit breakers handle the case where a call keeps failing — using only retries against a genuinely down service just multiplies the load on it and delays recovery.

When a Node service feels slow, or a specific endpoint has high latency that isn't explained by an obviously slow database query, the next step is CPU profiling rather than guessing. The built-in flag <strong>node --prof app.js</strong> records a V8 sampling profiler log while the app runs under real or simulated load; running <strong>node --prof-process</strong> on that log afterward produces a summary of where time was actually spent, broken down by JavaScript function, native code, and garbage collection — a high GC percentage often points to excessive object allocation rather than the code you'd naturally suspect. For a more visual view, tools like 0x or clinic flame from the clinic.js toolkit generate an interactive flame graph: wider bars represent functions that consumed more total CPU time including time spent in functions they called, making it easy to spot one unexpectedly hot function in a deep call stack. clinic.js also has clinic doctor, a first-pass diagnosis of whether the bottleneck is CPU, I/O, or event-loop delay. Chrome DevTools' Performance tab can also profile a running Node process directly via node --inspect, which is often the fastest way to go from symptom to a specific slow function without leaving a familiar UI.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// 1. Record a profile under load (use a load-testing tool like autocannon
//    against the running server in another terminal while this runs):
// node --prof server.js

// 2. Turn the raw log into a readable summary:
// node --prof-process isolate-0x*-v8.log > profile.txt

// 3. Or, for a visual flame graph:
// npx clinic flame -- node server.js

// A common finding in the summary output: a synchronous JSON.stringify()
// of a huge object on every request dominating the JavaScript percentage.
function buildResponse(hugeObject) {
  return JSON.stringify(hugeObject); // blocks the event loop for large objects
}
    </pre>
    Once the profile points at a specific function, the fix is usually one of: move the work off the main thread with worker_threads, reduce the amount of work with pagination or smaller payloads, or cache the result so it isn't recomputed on every request.

An <strong>uncaughtException</strong> fires when a synchronous error is thrown and nothing catches it anywhere up the call stack; an <strong>unhandledRejection</strong> fires when a Promise rejects and no catch handler, or try/catch around an await, ever handles it. Both indicate a bug — code assumed a path couldn't fail and didn't handle the case where it did. The Node.js docs are explicit that you should not try to resume normal operation after an uncaughtException: by the time it fires, the process may be in an inconsistent state, so continuing to serve traffic risks silent data corruption or increasingly strange failures. The recommended pattern is to log the error with full context, ideally to an external error-tracking service since the process may not survive long enough to flush a local log file, stop accepting new connections, let in-flight work finish if possible, and then exit, relying on a process manager or orchestrator to restart a fresh, clean process immediately after. unhandledRejection should be treated the same way in modern Node, since recent versions terminate the process by default for exactly this reason, rather than ignored, because an unhandled rejection is just as much a real bug as an uncaught throw.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
process.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {
  logger.fatal({ err }, 'Uncaught exception -- shutting down');
  // Stop accepting new work, let in-flight requests drain, then exit.
  server.close(() => process.exit(1));
  setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 5000).unref(); // hard fallback if close() hangs
});

process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason, promise) => {
  logger.fatal({ reason }, 'Unhandled promise rejection -- shutting down');
  // Treat exactly like uncaughtException -- this is a real, unhandled bug.
  server.close(() => process.exit(1));
  setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 5000).unref();
});
    </pre>
    These handlers are a safety net for logging and clean shutdown, not a substitute for proper error handling in the code itself — the goal is that they should rarely, ideally never, actually fire in practice.
